==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Hades the Deathdealer''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Almighty Hades''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Generally, Hades is used as a powerful tank to protect other units, built to be particularly useful on stages that mix traits, which would normally limit the usefulness of multiple tanks, as while they have more effective health than Hades in general, these units falter very quickly against enemies they are not meant to counter and when cursed. Hades attacks very fast and has short range, so he can easily wipe out weaker peons and stall other enemies consistently, but he's too slow to reach the frontline on time for this. His immunities can be situationally helpful, with his Warp Immunity helping you defeat [[Everlord Wanwan|Everlord WanWan]] and other [[:Category:Starred Alien Enemies|Starred Aliens]], while Toxic Immunity hard-counters a small number of admittedly few and not too hard [[Gobble]]/[[Zollow]] stages, as well as [[Saintly Sister (Merciless)|Papuu's Paradise]].

His rapid attacks are mostly detrimental to his role, as while this can get fragile enemies off him very quickly, enemies like Brollow and Rain D. get to abuse their high knockback count. He also has to remain there for an extended period to inflict damage comparable to good tankers, which reduces his reliability as he may not survive long enough to make an impact. His range and cooldown are both too much for a tank, and as such you may never get him on the field when you need him the most. Furthermore, his standing range of 250 means he won't be able to protect lower-ranged melee attackers.

==='''True Form'''===
Almighty Hades gains nothing but two immunities. Immunity to knockback is especially important for a tank, while immunity to slow can also be useful in some cases. Considering how late this True Form can be unlocked, due to the requirement of an Epic [[Catfruit]] Seed, it can be seen as a low cost, low reward trade. Immunity to knockback allows Hades to be extremely effective in stages that combine Long Distance attacks with knockback effects, namely [[Heavenly Tower/Floor 39|Floor 39]] of the [[Heavenly Tower]], where Elder Sloth and Mr. Mole will barely advance as Hades can remain in Tackey and Codename "Red Riding"'s blindspots, perhaps stalling long enough for another to arrive.

==='''Talents'''===
Since Almighty Hades relies on his stats to be useful, going for the extra attack and health is definitely a good idea, if one wishes to boost this unit with Catseyes. A defense [[Talent Orbs|Talent Orb]] can also be given to Hades to make him even tougher against a specific trait, possibly one that there is not a counter for among your Cats (excluding [[:Category:Traitless Enemies|Traitless]] enemies).

On the other hand, his slow and weaken talents are not as useful. Unlike your average specialist tanker or meatshield that have defensive abilities boosting their effective durability, these two abilities have low duration and activation chance, so they'll rarely be worthwhile. Furthermore, since they exclusively target Relics, the Curse Immunity talent is necessary for them to be practical. All of these talents are extremely expensive, and therefore not worth the investment due to the existence of far better alternatives which can counter Relics better.

==='''Ultra Talents'''===
Almighty Hades' Ultra Talents are self-explanatory, and as pointless as they are easy to understand. '''Cost Down''' seeks to resolve an issue that Hades never had, reducing his price tag by 27%. For such an inconsequential quality-of-life improvement, it is hardly worth the NP it asks of you. Cost Down still isn't bad per se, since you can get him out earlier alongside other attackers, and the amount of money it cuts is somewhat substantial. But when push comes to shove, it doesn't do anything to improve the unit in any meaningful way, or address his biggest problems in recharge rate or damage.

'''[[Immune to Surge|Surge Immunity]]''' is better, and improves matchups against a variety of Surge abusers like [[St. Dober]], [[Medu-san]], and the [[Surge Base (Enemy Base)|Surge Base]]. It makes his usage against Akus generally better, since many Aku stages tend to include a plethora of enemies with the Death Surge ability. But, as with all immunities, this is situational at best and shouldn't be the reason you invest into Hades' Ultra Talents.

In truth, the only reason you should be investing into Almighty Hades' Ultra Talents is for the additional Talent Orb slot. With it, you can double up on Defense Up Orbs, drastically increasing his effective HP against a trait of your choosing.

